    "content": "Title: \n\nURL Source: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/fa07c113-8958-4f1e-a010-0ef2bfc39e17/content\n\nPublished Time: Thu, 01 Oct 2026 08:37:11 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 58\n\nMarkdown Content:\nrsc.li/digitaldiscovery \u001f\u001e\u001d\u001c\u001d\u001b\u001a\u0019\u001f\u001f\u001e\u001d\u0018\u0017\u0016\u0015\u0014\u0013\u0012 rsc.li/digitaldiscovery ISSN 2635-098X Volume 1 Number 1 January 2022 \n\n# Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript \n\nThis is an Accepted Manuscript, which has been through the \n\nRoyal Society of Chemistry peer review process and has been accepted \n\nfor publication. \n\nAccepted Manuscripts are published online shortly after acceptance, \n\nbefore technical editing, formatting and proof reading. Using this free \n\nservice, authors can make their results available to the community, in \n\ncitable form, before we publish the edited article. We will replace this \n\nAccepted Manuscript with the edited and formatted Advance Article as \n\nsoon as it is available. \n\nYou can find more information about Accepted Manuscripts in the \n\nInformation for Authors .\n\nPlease note that technical editing may introduce minor changes to the \n\ntext and/or graphics, which may alter content. The journal’s standard \n\nTerms & Conditions  and the  Ethical  guidelines  still apply. In no event \n\nshall the Royal Society of Chemistry be held responsible for any errors \n\nor omissions in this Accepted Manuscript or any consequences arising \n\nfrom the use of any information it contains. Published on 29 September 2026 Licensed under CC-BY 4.0 Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids \n\nEgheosa Ogbomo, †,‡ Fionn Carman, ∗,†,‡ Daniele Dini, † and James P. Ewen ∗,†,¶\n\n†Department of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, U.K. \n\n‡These authors contributed equally \n\n¶Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Bath, Claverton Down, Bath BA2 7AY, U.K. \n\nE-mail: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk; j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk \n\n1 \n\n> Page 1 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nAbstract \n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C 10 –C 50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representa-tions. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products. \n\n2\n\nPage 2 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript Introduction \n\nThermophysical properties such as the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of organic molecules are important to the performance of liquid formulations such as lubricants and refrigerants. 1 Accurate prediction of these properties for pure organic molecules is also im-portant for a wide range of industries, including energy, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals. 2–4 \n\nIn this context, the lack of reliable thermophysical data is one of the largest stumbling blocks in rational molecular, 5 formulation, 6 and process 7 design. In particular, lubricants are required to adhere to strict viscosity limits across a wide temperature range (40–100 \n\n°C); 8 however, for the organic molecules relevant to this application (C 10 –C 50 ), 9 viscosity– temperature data are still lacking. Viscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to \n\n∼C24 , but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse. 10 There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C 10 –C 50 range. 11 \n\nIt is often possible to interpolate between missing entries with calculated predictions using a reliable equation of state, such as the perturbed-chain statistical associating fluid theory (PC-SAFT). 12,13 Several other predictive models are available for pure hydrocarbons, 14 with the most promising being group contribution and particularly entropy scaling methods. 15,16 \n\nQuantitative structure–property relationship (QSPR) models 17–21 have also been identified that are capable of predicting the viscosity of organic liquids. Within the chemical families and conditions for which they are parameterized, these methods can be highly accurate; their main limitation is reduced transferability, with predictive performance degrading out-side the molecules and conditions for which experimental data are available. 14 This makes extrapolation beyond the experimentally gathered dataset to new molecules or conditions less reliable. A quantitative comparison between these physically based methods and the machine learning models benchmarked here would require method-specific parameterization across the full dataset, and is left for future work. Over the last few decades, molecular dynamics (MD) simulations have become estab-3 \n\n> Page 3 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nlished as a useful tool with which to predict thermophysical properties and generate reliable datasets. 22 MD simulations can in principle be applied to any organic liquid for which the molecular structure is known and a suitable force field is available, with an accuracy set by the force field and the sampling. 23,24 The accuracy of force fields for MD simulations of the thermophysical properties of small molecules 25 and linear and branched hydrocarbons has recently been studied. 26 However, this type of comparison is still lacking for other industrially important classes of organic molecules, such as those containing cycloalkane, aromatic, ester, and ether groups. MD simulations are now routinely used to calculate the shear viscosity of single-component, 26–29 binary mixtures, 30–34 and multi-component 35–37 hydrocarbon flu-ids with high accuracy over a wide range of conditions. In most cases, viscosity calculations through MD simulations have been restricted to small molecules, typically linear or branched alkanes. This is because larger hydrocarbons generally have higher viscosities, 38 which are more challenging to accurately quantify through MD simulations due to their longer relax-ation times. 39 While MD simulations have also been used to predict the density and viscosity of oxygen-containing molecules 40,41 and their mixtures, 42 there are far fewer studies than for aliphatic hydrocarbons. Machine learning (ML) has also emerged as a useful tool for predicting the thermophysical properties of pure organic liquids 43 and their mixtures. 44 Accurate viscosity prediction has been achieved using ML models such as artificial neural networks for single-component, 45–51 \n\nbinary mixtures, 52 and multi-component 53,54 organic liquids. ML methods have also been shown to be effective in predicting the viscosity of more complex molecules, such as ionic liquids 55 and deep eutectic solvents. 56 As with QSPR models, the performance of ML models remains at the mercy of accurate training data availability, with small, sparse or noisy datasets all having adverse effects on model accuracy. Even the largest available datasets on the thermophysical properties of fluids, such as the NIST REFPROP database, 57 are sparse and subject to considerable uncertainties compared to other popular applications of ML. 43 \n\nSome recent studies have combined MD and ML by using dynamic descriptors from the 4\n\n> Page 4 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nMD simulations to improve ML predictions of various fluid properties. 58,59 In other cases, MD simulations have been used to generate reliable datasets with which to train ML models that can then be used for fluid property prediction. 60,61 MD simulations have also been used to generate large databases of viscosity values, which have then been used to optimize the molecular structure of lubricants with high viscosity index using ML methods. 5 A combina-tion of MD simulations and ML has also been used to facilitate the data-driven design of polymer-based viscosity index improver additives. 62 A recent study compiled a large experi-mental database containing viscosity values for 6,120 small organic liquids 59,63 and used this to train ML models to predict viscosity. 64 They found that the ML prediction models out-performed MD simulations across a range of molecules and temperatures. 64 A combination of ML and MD simulations was recently employed to predict the density, kinematic viscosity, thermal conductivity, specific heat capacity, and dielectric constant of esters in the context of immersion cooling fluids. 65 Both MD simulations 66 and ML models 67 have been considered for thermophysical property prediction for decades; however, only relatively recent advances in computational hardware and software have made this type of wide parameter study fea-sible. 22 However, systematic side-by-side evaluations of the two approaches on common sets of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules remain scarce. In this study, we benchmark ML models for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-relevant organic liquids, and use MD simulations on a representative subset as an independent, physics-based assessment of the same properties. The primary aim is to develop ML models suitable for high-throughput virtual screening 62,68 \n\nand molecular discovery 5,69 workflows for high-performance lubricants. The MD simulations serve a complementary purpose, probing where and why physics-based prediction succeeds or fails for these types of molecules. We evaluate 26 combinations of model architecture and molecular representation on approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values for 835 fuel and lubricant-sized molecules, to identify the most accurate combination for each property. Because screening 5 \n\n> Page 5 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nis most valuable beyond the chemistries that have already been measured, we then examine how far that accuracy extends to molecules unlike those seen in training, and which signals available at prediction time indicate where it does not. Finally, we apply Green–Kubo MD simulations to eight molecules, two from each of the four principal chemical families of the dataset, at 40 °C and 100 °C. This extends force field benchmarks that have concentrated on linear and branched alkanes 26,28,29 to alkyl cycloalkanes, alkyl aromatics, esters, and ethers, for which experimental viscosity data remain sparse 10 despite their importance in automotive fuels, 70 aviation fuels, 71 and lubricants. 9,72 \n\n## Methodology \n\nMachine Learning Models \n\nTo construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we col-lected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approxi-mately 1–50 mPa ·s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations. 39 Data were compiled from the Landolt–B¨ ornstein series 73 and the NIST Chemistry WebBook 74 (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values after the struc-ture canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below. In cases where the properties of interest were not provided exactly at 40 °C or 100 °C, interpolation was used to obtain the values at these temperatures. The NIST WebBook 74 uti-lizes a combination of property estimation methods to interpolate liquid viscosity values from the available experimental data. These include the PPDS9 (Physical Property Data Service) and ViscosityL correlations, which are based on semi-empirical and theoretical equations of state (extended Andrade models 75,76 ) fitted from experimental values. 4 The source method of every label is recorded, and the number of labels obtained by direct measurement versus 6\n\n> Page 6 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nby each database’s own correlation or reference-property model is reported by property and temperature in Table S2. Most of the labels for density and thermal conductivity (99.0% and 97.8% at 40 °C) and approximately 44% of the viscosity labels are values produced by the source databases’ own correlation, interpolation or reference-property models rather than direct measurements. The errors reported in this work therefore measure agreement with these adopted reference values, and do not include the uncertainty of the underlying database correlations. For molecules where both a direct measurement and a correlation value are available, the two agree closely across the full viscosity range at both temperatures (Figure S1). For interpolation of the liquid density values, the NIST WebBook uses the Tait equa-tion. 77 For liquid thermal conductivity, the TransportPolynomial model was applied, utilizing temperature-dependent polynomial fits to experimental thermal conductivity data. For the Landolt–B¨ ornstein series, 73 cubic spline interpolation using a minimum of three surrounding data points was employed to estimate the property values at the desired temperatures. In cases where only the kinematic viscosity was reported, it was converted to dynamic vis-cosity using μ = ν · ρ, where μ is dynamic viscosity, ν is kinematic viscosity, and ρ is density. Molecules whose viscosity was reported only as kinematic and for which no density was available at the same temperature were excluded (283 molecules at each temperature). Canonicalization and deduplication of the 1,187 source entries give 1,114 unique molecules, of which 835 retain at least one usable label and form the dataset used here. The viscosity values were normalized by taking their natural logarithm to improve regression performance during ML training. 54 \n\nA summary of the reference dataset is presented in Table 1. Of the 835 unique molecules, 637 (76%) are pure hydrocarbons containing only carbon and hydrogen, while the remaining 198 (24%) are oxygen-containing organic compounds such as esters and ethers. Oxygen-containing molecules were included alongside the pure hydrocarbons because oxygenated lubricant-like molecules, such as synthetic esters, 78 are an important part of the target ap-7 \n\n> Page 7 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nTable 1: Summary of the critically evaluated reference dataset compiled from the Landolt– B¨ ornstein series 73 and NIST Chemistry WebBook. 74 The dataset contains thermophysical properties for 835 unique organic molecules. \n\nProperty No. of compounds Min. value a Max. value a\n\nDensity (40 °C) 770 0.715 1.400 Density (100 °C) 769 0.668 1.340 Dynamic Viscosity (40 °C) 799 0.54 56.30 Dynamic Viscosity (100 °C) 804 0.29 22.00 Thermal Conductivity (40 °C) 785 0.054 0.276 Thermal Conductivity (100 °C) 786 0.051 0.250 \n\n> aUnits for density, viscosity and thermal conductivity are g ·cm −3, mPa ·s and W ·m−1·K−1, respectively.\n\nplication space, and both classes are represented in the MD benchmark below. The chemical family composition of the reference dataset is summarized in Table S1, with each molecule assigned to a single chemical family, as recorded in the released dataset. Some molecules may belong to more than one family due to the presence of multiple functional groups. In this study, we compare the most commonly used molecular representations on our reference dataset to see which enables ML models to perform best on transport property prediction tasks. Figure 1 shows the different molecular representations used to train ML models in this study. \n\nMolecular descriptors are numerical features that encode chemical, physical, and structural properties of molecules and are widely used for ML-based property prediction. 79,80 \n\nIn this study, descriptors were generated using the RDKit and Mordred Python libraries, 80 \n\nyielding 1,019 descriptors per molecule after removal of invalid, missing, and categorical features. Descriptor scaling was applied selectively based on model requirements. Z-score normalization was used for magnitude-sensitive models (SVM and k-NN), while no scaling was applied for tree-based models (random forest and XGBoost) to preserve the natural feature distributions and chemically meaningful thresholds. \n\nDimensionality-reduced embeddings provide a compact alternative to high-dimensional molecular descriptors by reducing noise and redundancy while retaining the most informa-8\n\n> Page 8 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nFigure 1: The molecular representations used to train the ML models, illustrated for buty-rophenone. tive features. 81 In this study, embeddings generated using principal component analysis (PCA) and uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP) were evaluated across a range of dimensionalities to assess their impact on model performance, as both the choice of method and retained dimensionality can influence predictive accuracy. PCA and UMAP embeddings were used as inputs to ML models to compare linear and nonlinear dimensional-ity reduction strategies for molecular property prediction. A detailed analysis of embedding method and dimensionality effects, including root-mean-square error (RMSE) trends across properties and model types, is provided in the Supporting Information. Embeddings were generated using the scikit-learn and UMAP-learn libraries from z-score normalized molecular 9 \n\n> Page 9 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\ndescriptors. \n\nMolecular fingerprints are typically represented as 2D binary strings, where each bit indicates the presence or absence of a specific structural feature or substructure within a molecule. A widely used and generally high-performing fingerprint type is the extended-connectivity fingerprint (ECFP), with the Morgan fingerprint being a common example. 82 \n\nIn this study, ECFPs are generated using the Morgan Fingerprint implementation in RD-Kit. 79 While prior work has examined optimal ECFP parameters for molecular similarity assessment, 83 no studies have specifically focused on optimizing ECFP generation for the property prediction of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules using ML models. Ac-cordingly, this study evaluates a range of ECFP generation parameters to identify those that yield the best regression performance. A systematic analysis of fingerprint parameters, including radius, bit size, and hashed versus unhashed representations, 83 is provided in the Supporting Information. \n\nMolecular graphs encode information about molecules by representing atoms as nodes and the bonds between them as edges. Nodes can be characterized by atom-level features such as atomic number, while the edges can incorporate bond-related characteristics such as bond type. Table S3 shows the features used to create molecular graphs in this study. \n\nSimplified molecular-input line-entry system (SMILES) strings encode molecu-lar structures as sequences of ASCII characters 84 but are not inherently unique, as multiple valid strings can represent the same molecule. Canonical SMILES address this by enforcing a deterministic representation, 85 although models trained on canonical SMILES may over-fit to syntactic patterns rather than underlying chemistry. 86 Despite this, SMILES remain widely used due to their simplicity and compatibility with sequence-based models such as CNNs and transformers. 87 SELFIES (Self-Referencing Embedded Strings) were introduced to guarantee syntactic validity, ensuring that any token sequence corresponds to a chemi-cally valid molecule. 88 In this study, both canonical SMILES and SELFIES were evaluated as input representations for molecular property prediction. Strings were converted to token 10 \n\n> Page 10 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nembeddings prior to model training; SMILES were generated from IUPAC names, 89 and SELFIES were generated using the published SELFIES API. 88 \n\nPretrained transformer embeddings were obtained from ChemBERTa, 90,91 a chem-istry language model pretrained on large SMILES corpora. The frozen ChemBERTa-77M-MTR encoder was used as a fixed feature extractor. Each molecule’s SMILES string is passed through the pretrained encoder and the token representations are mean-pooled (weighted by the attention mask) into a single fixed-length vector, with no fine-tuning of the encoder weights. These frozen embeddings were used as inputs to a multilayer perceptron (MLP) head and to an XGBoost model, providing a transfer-learning representation that comple-ments the descriptor-, fingerprint-, and string-based inputs above. In this study, we evaluate a range of models using the collated dataset to identify the most suitable algorithm for property prediction. The classic ML models, including sup-port vector machines (SVMs), 92 random forests (RFs), 93 and k-nearest neighbors ( k-NN), 94 \n\nare implemented using the scikit-learn package in Python. 95 XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) models were trained using the XGBoost Python library. 96 The graph-based mod-els, message passing neural networks (MPNNs) 97 and graph attention networks (GATs), 98 \n\nare implemented with the PyTorch Geometric library. 99 The deep learning models, con-volutional neural networks (CNNs), 100 multilayer perceptrons, 101 and transformers 87 were implemented with the PyTorch library. 99 ChemBERTa 90,91 was evaluated in two configura-tions using the HuggingFace Transformers library: fine-tuning the full pretrained encoder end-to-end on SMILES, and the frozen-embedding configuration described above, in which only a small MLP head is trained on the fixed mean-pooled encoder output. All models were evaluated under a single shared nested cross-validation protocol, ap-plied to molecular representation configurations that were fixed in advance. The embedding dimensionality and the fingerprint radius and bit size were chosen by grid search on a sin-gle 80:10:10 train/validation/test split, as reported in the Supporting Information, so the protocol is nested with respect to hyperparameter selection and conditional on those repre-11  \n\n> Page 11 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nsentation configurations. The dataset was partitioned into five fixed outer folds, so that both temperatures of a given molecule always fall in the same fold and no molecule appears in more than one outer test set. The folds were constructed by stratified sampling over quintiles of the log-transformed 40 °C viscosity labels (molecules without a viscosity label were assigned via median-filled strata), then frozen. For each outer fold, hyperparameters were selected by random search over 25 configurations sampled from model-specific ranges, scored on a designated validation partition of the non-test data (approximately 85:15 train/validation); the selected configuration was then retrained on all non-test data and evaluated once on the untouched outer test fold. Full details of the architectures, hyperparameter ranges, and training procedures for each model are provided in the Supporting Information. \n\nMolecular Dynamics Simulations \n\nFigure 2: The eight MD target molecules, two from each principal chemical family of lubricant base oils in the dataset: alkanes (squalane, nonylcyclohexane), alkyl aromatics (4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene), esters (decyl stearate, bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate), and polyethers (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane, tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether). The molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in Figure 2. These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical fami-lies encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software. 102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 ◦C and 100 ◦C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values. 8 All 12 \n\n> Page 12 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nsimulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field. 103 Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen et al. 104 and Siu et al. 103 Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed, 105 while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because pre-vious benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including \n\nn-hexadecane, 28 n-dodecane, and n-octacosane. 29 \n\nInitial single molecule geometries were generated from the SMILES strings in RDKit by adding explicit hydrogens, embedding with the ETKDGv3 distance geometry algorithm, 106 \n\nand minimizing with UFF. Cubic starting configurations were assembled with Packmol 107 \n\nat a low initial density (0.3 g/cm 3), with molecule counts selected to give approximately 15,000 atoms per box (163–349 molecules, depending on molecular size), corresponding to equilibrated cubic box lengths of 5.2–5.5 nm. A dedicated system size sensitivity study was not performed here, since all systems are well above the range where finite-size effects would be expected to affect the viscosity 39 and thermal conductivity. 108 Periodic boundary conditions were applied in all three Cartesian directions. The protocol suggested by Schmitt et al. 26 was adopted in the EMD simulations because it has been shown to enable accurate calculation of the viscosity of lubricant-sized molecules. A velocity–Verlet integrator with a 1.0 fs timestep was employed. 109 Bonds containing hydrogen atoms were constrained with the SHAKE algorithm. 110 Constraining C–H bonds is important for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities with all-atom force fields, as it suppresses the classically over-excited high-frequency C–H stretching modes that otherwise inflate the Green–Kubo heat flux. 111 Lennard–Jones interactions were truncated at 12 ˚ A with long-range tail corrections applied to energy and pressure, and long-range electrostatics were treated using the particle–particle particle–mesh (PPPM) method 112 with a relative accuracy of 10 −5. Geometric mixing rules were used for the unlike-atom interactions, and intramolecular 1–4 interactions were scaled by 0.5 following the OPLS convention. 13  \n\n> Page 13 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nThe system was first relaxed by conjugate gradient energy minimization, followed by a short displacement-capped Langevin dynamics stage to remove any remaining close contacts. An equilibration in the canonical (NVT) ensemble was then performed at the target temper-ature to allow the system to reach thermal equilibrium. The density was then equilibrated at 1.0 atm and the target temperature for 1.0 ns in the isothermal-isobaric (NPT) ensemble using the Nos´ e–Hoover thermostat and barostat 113,114 with damping coefficients of 100 fs and 1000 fs, respectively. The density was obtained directly as the ensemble averaged mass density at atmospheric pressure over the final 0.25 ns of the NPT trajectory, where a steady-state value had been reached. 26 Density uncertainties were estimated as five-block standard deviations over this averaging window and are at most 0.003 g cm −3 (≤0.4%) for every state point. The simulation box was then deformed isotropically over 0.5 ns at constant temper-ature until the fluid density matched this average value. For each molecule, 10 independent replicas were branched from the equilibrated configuration with independently seeded veloc-ities, each given a 0.2 ns NVT warm-up before production. Production was carried out in the NVT ensemble for 8 ns per replica to allow the slow shear-stress autocorrelation tail to converge. 26 \n\nViscosity was calculated using the Green–Kubo (GK) formalism, 115,116 which is well suited to low and moderate viscosity fluids ( < 50 mPa s). 39 The GK expression for the shear viscosity is: \n\nη = VkB T\n\nZ ∞\n\n> 0\n\n⟨σαβ (t0)σαβ (t + t0)⟩t0 dt (1) where η is the viscosity, V is the system volume, kB is the Boltzmann constant, T is the temperature, and σαβ (t) is an off-diagonal component of the stress tensor at time t (with \n\nα and β denoting Cartesian directions x, y, or z); the angle brackets denote the stress autocorrelation function (ACF) averaged over time origins. To maximize the statistical sampling, the viscosity was averaged over all six independent components of the pressure tensor, combining the three off-diagonal terms with the three diagonal-difference terms, as recommended by Maginn et al. 39 \n\n14 \n\n> Page 14 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nThe time decomposition method (TDM) of Zhang et al. 117 was used to improve the reliability of the calculations for the high-viscosity molecules. The time evolution of the running integral η(t) was recorded over the full production trajectory of each replica, with the stress ACF evaluated using a fast Fourier transform (FFT) technique. 118 A cutoff time \n\ntcut was applied to the integral to mitigate accumulation of long-time noise, determined from the ratio of the standard deviation ση(t) across replicas to the average running viscosity: 117 \n\nση\n\nηfinal \n\n= 0 .4matching the criterion of Schmitt et al., 26 who showed that ratios of ση/η final between 0.2 and 0.8 do not materially affect the extracted values. The bootstrapping method, 119 with 200 iterations, was used to compute the final viscosity value and its uncertainty, following the time-decomposition procedure of the PyLaT software 120 and implemented in the released analysis script. Random combinations of the 10 replicas were resampled with replacement, and for each combination the replica-averaged running viscosity ¯ η(t) was fitted with the double-exponential function of Eq. 2, in which η∞ is the long-time viscosity limit and α, β1,and β2 are fitting parameters: \n\nη(t) = η∞\n\n\u0002αβ 1\n\n\u00001 − e−t/β 1\n\n\u0001 + (1 − α)β2\n\n\u00001 − e−t/β 2\n\n\u0001\u0003 \n\nαβ 1 + (1 − α)β2\n\n(2) This functional form reflects the physics of the underlying stress autocorrelation function, which decays through two distinct regimes: a fast relaxation associated with local, high-frequency stress fluctuations and a slow relaxation governed by collective, long-time struc-tural rearrangement. 117 The two exponential terms, with time constants β1 and β2 and relative weight α, capture these two modes, so that the running integral η(t) rises from zero and saturates toward the plateau value η∞, which is taken as the converged viscosity. Fit-ting to this form allows the well-sampled short-time behavior to determine η∞ while avoiding the accumulation of statistical noise in the poorly converged long-time tail of the integral, 15  \n\n> Page 15 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nthereby providing a more robust estimate than reading off the running integral directly. 39,117 \n\nThe Green–Kubo formalism was also used to compute the thermal conductivity κ,121,122 \n\nfrom the autocorrelation of the microscopic heat flux vector Jq:\n\nκ = 13V k B T 2\n\nZ ∞\n\n> 0\n\n⟨Jq(t0) · Jq(t + t0)⟩t0 dt (3) where κ is the thermal conductivity, Jq is the heat flux vector, the factor of three averages over the Cartesian components of the heat flux, and the angle brackets indicate a time-origin average of the autocorrelation function. The microscopic heat current was evaluated in LAMMPS using the centroid-based per-atom stress formulation of Surblys et al. 123 for the pair, bond, angle, dihedral, and improper virial contributions. This formulation treats the many-body bonded interactions consistently in the heat-current expression, which is essential for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities in molecular systems with angle, dihedral, and improper terms. As with the viscosity, the thermal conductivity was obtained from the running Green– Kubo integral κ(t), with the heat-flux ACF averaged over the three Cartesian components and evaluated by FFT. 118 Unlike the shear stress, however, the heat-flux ACF carries no slow tail. The ACF decays to zero within a few picoseconds, so the running integral κ(t)reaches an early plateau and then drifts as statistical noise accumulates in the long-time tail. The double-exponential TDM fit used for the viscosity is therefore not required for the thermal conductivity, and integrating κ(t) to long lag times would only accumulate noise; the uncertainty of the Green–Kubo estimate grows with the upper integration limit relative to the total trajectory length, 39 so the conductivity must be read from the short-time plateau rather than the converged integral. To locate this plateau without a hand-chosen cutoff, we first identified the lag time t∗ at which the normalized heat-flux ACF decays into its noise floor, defined as the first point beyond which its magnitude remains below twice the standard deviation of the long-time, noise-dominated tail. The thermal conductivity was 16 \n\n> Page 16 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nthen taken as the average of κ(t) over the window [ t∗, t ∗ + 10 ps], immediately after the autocorrelation has decayed and before long-time noise sets in, with the reported value and uncertainty given by the mean and standard deviation of this window average across the 10 independent replicas. \n\n## Results and Discussion \n\nComparing ML Models \n\nPrior to benchmarking, we investigated embedding and fingerprinting strategies by system-atic grid search on a single 80:10:10 train/validation/test split, with full details provided in the Supporting Information (Figure S2 - Figure S5, Table S4 - Table S5). For embeddings, PCA consistently outperformed UMAP across most properties and model types; one hypoth-esis is that its linear variance-preserving transformations suit the axis-aligned splits used by tree-based models, though the benchmark does not test this directly. Based on these results, PCA embeddings of 100 dimensions were used for all models. For fingerprints, increasing radius and bit size improved performance despite higher bit-collision rates, and unhashed Morgan fingerprints with a radius of 8 were selected as they eliminated collision-induced ambiguity and yielded the best overall predictive performance. Both comparisons rest on that single split and were not repeated within each outer fold, so they rank embedding and fingerprint configurations under one split rather than under the nested protocol. All 26 model–representation combinations were then evaluated under the shared nested cross-validation protocol described in the Methods, using the same fixed outer folds for every model, representation and property. Conditional on the representation configurations fixed above, the quoted test errors are therefore free of test-set-tuned hyperparameter selection, although the outer folds serve as a comparative evaluation across the 26 combinations rather than a fully untouched holdout for the single winning model. All metrics reported below are means over the five outer folds, with the between-fold standard deviation as an estimate of 17  \n\n> Page 17 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\ntheir variability. Table S6 reports the mean test RMSE and R2 for every model–representation combina-tion, property and temperature; per-fold metrics, including mean absolute error (MAE) and the between-fold standard deviations, are provided with the released code. To identify the strongest models we z-score normalize the per-property RMSE values, invert them so that lower error corresponds to higher performance, and average across properties; we use this ranking only to select a common set of leading models and base all substantive claims on the property-specific results. Figure 3 shows how the five best-ranked models perform for each property at 40 °C (a–c) and 100 °C (d–f). Here “PCA embeddings” refers to the 100-dimensional PCA of molecular descriptors described above, whereas “ChemBERTa embeddings” refers to the fixed mean-pooled vectors produced by the pretrained ChemBERTa encoder. 90,91 \n\nXGBoost and RF models, when used with molecular descriptors, performed most con-sistently across all three transport properties. XGBoost with descriptors gave the low-est viscosity error of any combination (RMSE = 0 .35 and 0 .30 in ln( η/ mPa s) at 40 and 100 °C, R2 = 0.86 and 0 .83) and was among the best two or three combinations for density and thermal conductivity, while RF with descriptors gave the lowest density er-ror (RMSE = 0 .047 g cm −3). An error of 0 .35 on the logarithmic scale corresponds to a typical discrepancy of a factor of 1 .4 between predicted and measured viscosity. This aligns with previous studies, 124,125 which also found tree-based models to be reliable across datasets. These methods handle nonlinear relationships and high-dimensional features effec-tively, making them suitable for the diverse information captured by molecular descriptors, and are relatively insensitive to feature scale, irregular target functions and uninformative features. 126 \n\nWhen comparing representations for XGBoost, overall predictive performance followed the trend descriptors > PCA embeddings > ChemBERTa embeddings > fingerprints, and this ordering is particularly pronounced for viscosity, where the fingerprint model was sub-18 \n\n> Page 18 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nFigure 3: The five best-ranked ML model–representation combinations for each property at 40 °C (a–c) and 100 °C (d–f): (a, d) viscosity, modeled as ln( η/ mPa s), (b, e) density and (c, f) thermal conductivity. Bars are mean test RMSE over the five locked outer folds, ordered by rank, with between-fold standard deviations as error bars. “PCA embeddings” denotes the 100-dimensional PCA of molecular descriptors and “ChemBERTa embeddings” the frozen mean-pooled ChemBERTa vectors. Results for all 26 combinations are given in Table S6. stantially weaker (RMSE = 0 .67 and 0 .55; R2 = 0 .51 and 0 .42). This may be explained by the fact that while descriptors and PCA embeddings can incorporate both structural and physicochemical information, fingerprints are purely structural, lacking explicit encoding of 19  \n\n> Page 19 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nglobal molecular properties, which limits their expressiveness for the transport properties considered here. The SVM and k-NN models with molecular descriptors also achieved accuracy close to the leading tree-based models. SVM with descriptors reached a viscosity RMSE of 0 .38 and 0.32 ( R2 = 0 .84 and 0 .80), while k-NN with descriptors was clearly weaker at 0 .44 and 0 .36. For these distance-based models, descriptor and PCA embedding inputs gave comparable accuracy (for example, SVM viscosity RMSE of 0 .38/0 .32 with descriptors versus 0 .38/0 .31 with PCA embeddings). Some sensitivity to the high dimensionality and redundancy of descriptor sets is expected for distance-based methods, 127 and may explain their larger errors relative to the tree-based models. The GAT and MPNN models, trained on molecular graphs augmented with three-dimensional geometric information (bond lengths and partial charges derived from ETKDG conformers), were the weakest combinations, with GAT giving a viscosity RMSE of 0 .78 and 0.63 ( R2 = 0 .33 and 0 .22). This is notable given the theoretical advantages of geometric in-formation for modeling molecular properties, and that graph ML methods have shown strong performance on related tasks. 52,128 Two factors plausibly contribute: graph models typically require larger datasets than the one used here, 129 and ETKDG conformers, 106 while inexpen-sive, do not always capture the most energetically favorable geometry of flexible molecules, so the bond lengths and partial charges may only approximate the environments that govern transport properties. More accurate conformer generation, using density functional theory or machine-learning force fields, is therefore one route to improvement. Within this study the MPNN outperformed the GAT for every property and temperature, by 19 and 26% in viscosity RMSE at 40 and 100 °C and by 4 to 15% for density and thermal conductivity, on identical graphs and under the same search budget, although both remained well behind the descriptor-based models. Among the deep-learning models, the transformer trained on the PCA molecular em-beddings fell in the lower third of the benchmark ( η RMSE 0 .53 and 0 .39; R2 = 0 .69 and 20 \n\n> Page 20 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\n0.70), well behind the MLP trained on the same embeddings (0 .43 and 0 .37), so the shortfall lies with the architecture rather than the representation. The two ChemBERTa 91 config-urations behaved very differently. Used as a frozen feature extractor, with only a small MLP head trained on the fixed mean-pooled embedding of each molecule, ChemBERTa was one of the strongest models overall. It gave the lowest thermal-conductivity error at 40 °C (RMSE = 0 .0095 W m −1 K−1, R2 = 0 .89) and consistent performance across all three properties, with viscosity accuracy (RMSE = 0 .41 and 0 .34) moderately behind the leading descriptor models. By contrast, fine-tuning the full ChemBERTa encoder end-to-end on SMILES predicted viscosity somewhat more accurately (RMSE = 0 .37 and 0 .31) but was markedly less consistent, with substantially larger density and thermal-conductivity errors (ρ R 2 = 0 .51). This pattern suggests that, on a dataset of this size, updating all encoder weights can overwrite useful pretrained features, whereas freezing them and learning only a lightweight head preserves the transferable representation and generalizes better. A chemistry-pretrained transformer was thus among the best models for these trans-port properties when its pretrained embeddings were used directly. The comparatively poor SMILES fine-tuning is consistent with a distributional mismatch between ChemBERTa’s predominantly drug-like pretraining corpus 91,130 and the lubricant-sized molecules studied here, and points to parameter-efficient fine-tuning such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), 131 \n\nwhich freezes the base weights and introduces small trainable matrices, as a promising al-ternative, as demonstrated for other large chemistry and protein language models. 132 \n\nModels trained on character-level embeddings of string-based molecular representations such as SMILES and SELFIES generally underperformed relative to models using molecular descriptors or PCA embeddings, though the best string-based models (the CNN and trans-former) were only moderately behind the leading combinations for viscosity. For a given architecture, SMILES inputs matched SELFIES for viscosity (for example, CNN RMSE of 0.45/0 .35 for SMILES versus 0 .45/0 .36 for SELFIES) and tended to outperform them for density and thermal conductivity, despite the theoretical advantages of SELFIES in guaran-21  \n\n> Page 21 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nteeing valid molecular strings. This performance gap may stem from differences in how the two formats encode structure. While SMILES strings are not guaranteed to produce valid molecules when randomly generated or mutated, 85 they tend to preserve local structural pat-terns such as rings, branches and functional groups, so that substructures that are adjacent in chemical space often remain close in the string; this implicit locality may help character-level models learn chemically meaningful sub-patterns. SELFIES strings, by contrast, are designed always to decode to valid molecules, 88 but the encoding can disrupt local chemical continuity, place related substructures far apart in the sequence, and produce longer strings with larger token vocabularies, all of which can make it harder for convolutional or recurrent models to learn useful patterns from token proximity. Throughout, these comparisons char-acterize the particular implementations, feature choices and tuning budget evaluated here under a common protocol, rather than the model classes in general; the graph and sequence models were the most sensitive to those choices in our hands. The property-specific R2 values in Table S6 reveal several further trends across the leading models. Density has the lowest R2 of the three properties for every model ( R2 ≤ 0.77), but this reflects the narrow spread of the density labels rather than poor accuracy: the best model reaches 0 .047 g cm −3, about 5% of the mean value and the smallest relative error of the three properties. For thermal conductivity and viscosity, moderate-to-high R2 values are observed, with the descriptor-based tree models and, for thermal conductivity, the frozen ChemBERTa embeddings performing best. These trends highlight how both temperature and feature representation affect regression accuracy across the different thermophysical properties. The viscosity results are also robust to the source method of the training labels. Retrain-ing the XGBoost descriptor models using only the explicitly experimental viscosity labels, with the same locked folds and selected hyperparameters, left the error on directly measured test labels unchanged at 40 °C (RMSE 0.277 versus 0.272) and marginally lower at 100 °C(0.209 versus 0.225), well within the fold-to-fold variability (Table S7). The recovered corre-22 \n\n> Page 22 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\nlation and interpolation labels, approximately 44% of the viscosity training data, therefore neither inflate nor degrade accuracy on measured data. For thermal conductivity and density an equivalent check is not possible, because all recovered labels for these properties derive from the source databases’ correlation models; the reported errors therefore measure agreement with those critically evaluated reference values. The associated temperature interpolation is, however, far better conditioned than for viscosity. The median change between 40 °C and 100 °C across the dataset is 4.4% for density and 5.9% for thermal conductivity, compared with a median factor of 3.5 for viscosity. \n\nOut-of-Distribution Prediction \n\nIt is well-known that ML models yield unreliable property predictions for molecules that lie outside the chemical domain of their training sets. 133,134 This limitation poses signifi-cant challenges for molecular discovery pipelines, 135 where candidate compounds frequently occupy regions of chemical space not sampled during training, and experimental measure-ments for such molecules are scarce. The severity of the problem depends on representation. Fingerprint-based models encode only substructures seen during training, so novel fragments activate no informative bits, while string-based tokenizations rely on sequential relation-ships that out-of-distribution atoms and functional groups distort. Molecular descriptors vary smoothly with structural modification and encode continuous global quantities that can change more gradually than sparse substructure indicators, making them comparatively better suited to extrapolation. 136 Tossou et al. characterized out-of-distribution (OOD) pre-diction as a covariate shift problem and showed that performance degradation depends more on representation than on choice of model, with RDKit descriptors minimizing it. 137 \n\nQuantifying OOD status is itself difficult. Previous studies have used Tanimoto simi-larity on fingerprints 138 or Euclidean distance in descriptor space, 137 but novel compounds trigger near-zero fingerprint similarity, and Euclidean distance can be dominated by a few 23  \n\n> Page 23 of 57 Digital Discovery Digital Discovery Accepted Manuscript\n\n\n...60520 bytes truncated...\n\nThe tool call succeeded but the output was truncated. Full output saved to: /home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d44c052001BcpLlClHTkU7B5\nUse Grep to search the full content or Read with offset/limit to view specific sections."
